4. Corrected Storage Migration and Rollback Procedure
4.1 Why Switching OBJECT_STORAGE_PROVIDER from r2 to local_bucket Is Unsafe
Once R2 is active on Cloud Run:
Cloud Run instances scale up/down or restart on deployments, wiping .object-storage-bucket/.
Even on a long-lived instance, uploads handled by Instance A are not present in Instance B's .object-storage-bucket/.
Therefore, switching OBJECT_STORAGE_PROVIDER=local_bucket in production would cause immediate 404 OBJECT_NOT_FOUND errors for every object whose bytes are only in R2 (and would also violate the production fail-closed guard on ephemeral local storage).
4.2 Production-Safe Migration & Rollback Architecture
Phase A — Forward Migration (local_bucket / arvancloud → r2)
Run WP-0 (Existing Data Migration Assessment, Section 8) to inventory all active metadata records and byte locations.
Backfill Existing Bytes to R2:
For every active object in media_object_metadata_v2 / index.json not yet in R2, copy bytes to R2 under its canonical internalObjectKey, verify SHA-256 and sizeBytes, and update MediaMetadataRecord (providerId: 'r2', bucket: <r2-bucket>, byteStorageVerified: true).
Dual-Read Capability by Object Record (providerId + bucket per record):
Retrieval (GET /api/object-storage/file/:objectId) resolves the storage target from the object's own MediaMetadataRecord.providerId and MediaMetadataRecord.internalObjectKey, with fallback read-through from R2 whenever local cache is cold.
Phase B — What Happens When R2 Becomes Unavailable
Uploads (POST /api/object-storage/upload, /upload-complete): Fail closed with 502 REMOTE_STORAGE_WRITE_FAILED (retryable: true). Never silently write to ephemeral local disk in production.
Downloads (GET /api/object-storage/file/:objectId):
If the requested object happens to be warm in the serving Cloud Run instance's local .object-storage-bucket/ cache and its size/hash matches MediaMetadataRecord, it is served from the local read cache (200 / 206).
If the local cache is cold and R2 GET fails/times out, return 503 STORAGE_PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE (Cache-Control: no-store, Retry-After: 30) — never 404 OBJECT_NOT_FOUND (which would falsely tell clients/browsers/CDNs that the media was deleted).
Phase C — Which Rollback Procedures Are Actually Safe in Production
SAFE Rollback Option 1: Code/Revision Rollback while Retaining R2 Bucket
Rolling back a Cloud Run revision while keeping OBJECT_STORAGE_PROVIDER=r2 (and R2 credentials) intact is safe because both revisions read/write the same durable R2 bucket and Firestore media_object_metadata_v2 collection.
SAFE Rollback Option 2: Provider Rollback (r2 → s3_compatible e.g. ArvanCloud/Liara)
Precondition: Must first run a bounded hydration/backfill sync job copying all objects created during the R2 window (providerId === 'r2') to the target S3-compatible bucket and verifying SHA-256 digests + byte sizes against media_object_metadata_v2.
Dual-Read Window: Enable dual-read (check primary provider; on 404, read from secondary R2 provider) before switching the write provider.
Completion: Only switch OBJECT_STORAGE_PROVIDER after missingInTargetCount === 0 is verified.
Realistic Timeframe: Proportional to data volume (e.g., 5–30 minutes for up to MAX_TOTAL_STORAGE_BYTES = 2 GB), never promised as sub-60-second or zero-downtime without pre-replicated bytes.
UNSAFE / FORBIDDEN Rollback:
Switching production OBJECT_STORAGE_PROVIDER from r2 back to local_bucket without a shared persistent volume is forbidden in production because container restarts lose all local bytes.
