How Express trust proxy = N interacts with X-Forwarded-For on Cloud Run + Cloudflare:
Express's proxy-addr counts N socket/proxy hops from the right end of X-Forwarded-For.
Direct to Cloud Run (DNS-Only / Grey Cloud):
Client sends spoofed X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4.
Google Front End (GFE / Cloud Run ingress) appends the real client IP 203.0.113.50 → X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4, 203.0.113.50, and connects to the container via an internal socket IP (169.254.x.x).
With trustProxyHops = 1, Express trusts 1 hop (the internal socket) and selects the rightmost entry in X-Forwarded-For (203.0.113.50).
CRITICAL DANGER: If TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2 is set while traffic bypasses Cloudflare (or if a client connects directly to the *.run.app URL!), Express steps back 2 hops and selects the attacker-supplied 1.2.3.4, allowing trivial rate-limit and abuse-cooldown bypass!
Cloudflare (Orange Cloud) → Cloud Run:
Client sends spoofed X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4.
Cloudflare appends the real client IP 203.0.113.50 (X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4, 203.0.113.50) and sets CF-Connecting-IP: 203.0.113.50.
GFE appends the Cloudflare edge egress IP 172.68.x.x (X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4, 203.0.113.50, 172.68.x.x).
Here, only if the penultimate hop (172.68.x.x) is a verified Cloudflare IP range is 203.0.113.50 trustworthy.
7.2 Mandatory Proxy Verification & Anti-Spoofing Protocol
Empirical Hop Verification Before Changing TRUST_PROXY_HOPS:
Keep TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1 as the safe default on Cloud Run until Cloudflare Orange-Cloud proxy is active AND direct *.run.app bypass is blocked or verified.
Verify the exact X-Forwarded-For hop structure in staging by sending a request with a synthetic header curl -H "X-Forwarded-For: 198.51.100.99" https://<domain>/api/healthz and confirming getClientIp(req) resolves to the real client IP and never 198.51.100.99.
Automated IP Spoofing Regression Tests (tests/delivery-and-abuse-guard.test.ts):
Test Case 1 (TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1, direct Cloud Run): Request with socket.remoteAddress = '169.254.1.1' and X-Forwarded-For: '1.1.1.1, 203.0.113.7' MUST resolve getClientIp(req) === '203.0.113.7' (ignoring spoofed 1.1.1.1).
Test Case 2 (Direct *.run.app access when TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2 without Cloudflare IP validation): Demonstrate why TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2 is unsafe unless the hop added by GFE is verified to belong to Cloudflare IPv4/IPv6 CIDR ranges (or Authenticated Origin Pulls / Cloud Run ingress restriction is active).
7.3 Exact Origin TLS, Hostname, DNS, and Cloud Run Domain-Mapping Prerequisites & Realistic Rollback Timing
Before any Cloudflare cutover occurs, the following 5 infrastructure prerequisites must be established:
Cloud Run Custom Domain Mapping or HTTPS Load Balancer:
Cloud Run rejects requests whose Host header does not match a mapped custom domain or the service's *.run.app hostname unless Rewrite Host Header (Cloudflare Enterprise/Origin Rules) or Cloud Run Custom Domain Mapping (radartehran.ir) is configured and its Google-managed certificate is active.
Origin TLS Certificate & SSL/TLS Mode:
Cloudflare SSL/TLS encryption mode MUST be Full (Strict) so Cloudflare validates the Cloud Run certificate on 443. Never use Flexible (which sends HTTP to Cloud Run and causes an infinite redirect loop with resolveCanonicalRedirectUrl in src/utils/serverDeliveryAndAbuseGuard.ts:81-108 and Cloud Run's own HTTP→HTTPS redirect).
APP_URL Alignment:
APP_URL in Cloud Run environment variables must match the canonical public https://<domain> hostname because resolveCanonicalRedirectUrl() (src/utils/serverDeliveryAndAbuseGuard.ts:103-107) issues a 308 Permanent Redirect if www.<canonicalHost> or http:// is used.
CSP Nonce Preservation:
Cloudflare Rocket Loader, Auto Minify (HTML), and Email Address Obfuscation must be Disabled so HTML responses with dynamic SEO tags (injectDynamicSeoMeta in server.ts:252) and CSP headers are not mutated at the edge.
Realistic DNS / Proxy Rollback Timing:
Do not promise sub-60-second rollback for DNS changes.
Switching a Cloudflare DNS record from Proxied (Orange Cloud) to DNS-Only (Grey Cloud) propagates at Cloudflare authoritative nameservers within ~1–5 minutes, plus downstream recursive resolver TTL (set DNS TTL to 60s or Auto prior to cutover; some ISP resolvers cache for 5–15 minutes).
If a nameserver (NS) rollback at the .ir registry (IRNIC) is required, propagation takes 4 to 24 hours.
